<BaseLayout title="Editorial standards — FrontierAGI Academy">
  <article class="prose container"><p class="eyebrow">Trust and transparency</p><h1>Editorial standards</h1><p>Frontier AI changes quickly. Every published lesson should make it easy to distinguish durable explanation from time-sensitive reporting.</p><h2>Required article metadata</h2><ul><li>Named author and reviewer</li><li>Publication and last-verified dates</li><li>Difficulty, prerequisites and learning outcomes</li><li>Primary sources for consequential factual claims</li><li>Clear labels for facts, estimates, allegations and opinions</li><li>An uncertainty and omissions section</li></ul><h2>Source policy</h2><p>Prefer research papers, official technical reports, regulatory text and first-party documentation. Secondary reporting may establish context but should not silently replace primary evidence.</p><h2>Corrections</h2><p>Material corrections should be visible, dated and described. Updating an article must not erase the history of a substantive error.</p><h2>AI assistance</h2><p>AI-assisted research or drafting must be reviewed by a responsible human editor before publication. Generated citations must be verified against the underlying source.</p></article>
</BaseLayout>
